5. Thermodynamics
The first and second laws of thermodynamics apply to all living organisms. A living organism represents an open thermodynamic system that continuously exchanges compounds and heat with the environment, performs mechanical work, and disposes of internal entropy production. The first law was first tested in organisms by Max Rubner who in 1889 kept an adult dog in a calorimeter for 45 days measuring all input and output flows (food, feces, urine, and


